artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the dynamics of N point vortices on a two-dimensional Riemannian manifold M (possibly with boundary, possibly non-orientable). The motion of N vortices with strengths Γ_1,...,Γ_N at positions x_1,...,x_N ∈ M is governed by the Hamiltonian system
   Γ_k (dx_k/dt) = J ∇_{x_k} H(x_1,...,x_N),
where H is built from the hydrodynamic Green's function G_M of the Laplace–Beltrami operator on M with appropriate boundary conditions (e.g., no-flux on ∂M):
   H(x_1,...,x_N) = Σ_{j<k} Γ_j Γ_k G_M(x_j, x_k) + (1/2) Σ_k Γ_k^2 R_M(x_k),
with R_M the Robin function (the regular part of G_M on the diagonal). For non-orientable surfaces, the construction uses the orientable double cover and image vortices.

For each of the following manifolds M, and for small numbers of vortices N (typically N = 2, 3, 4):

   (a) the half-plane {(x,y) : y > 0};
   (b) the quarter-plane {(x,y) : x > 0, y > 0};
   (c) the hemisphere;
   (d) the spherical quarter (a fundamental domain of the sphere under a Z/2 × Z/2 reflection group);
   (e) the unit disk;
   (f) a flat torus R^2 / Λ for a lattice Λ;
   (g) a finite or infinite flat cylinder;
   (h) a half-cylinder (cylinder with one boundary circle),

carry out a detailed study of the resulting dynamical system: classify relative equilibria and fixed configurations, determine their linear and nonlinear stability, describe the structure of the energy–momentum map and the foliation by invariant tori, identify and analyze the bifurcations of relative equilibria and periodic orbits as the vortex strengths, the positions, and (where applicable) the geometric parameters of M (aspect ratio, lattice shape, radius) are varied, and characterize integrability and the appearance of chaotic regimes.

